      {
        "narration": "As the legend goes, every single resident vanished from an Inuit village in 1930. Fur trapper Joe Labelle paddled to the shores of Lake Anjikuni, expecting a bustling community.",
        "visual_query": "canadian arctic lake shore",
        "visual_fallback": "frozen lake landscape"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Instead, he found absolute silence. Unfinished shirts still had sewing needles in them, and food hung over unlit fire pits, left as if mid-task.",
        "visual_query": "rustic log cabin interior",
        "visual_fallback": "abandoned wooden cabin"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Even more unsettling, seven sled dogs were found dead from starvation, and a grave appeared to have been opened without disturbing the circling stones. How does an entire settlement simply evaporate?",
        "visual_query": "snowy winter wilderness trail",
        "visual_fallback": "snowy landscape"
      },
      {
        "narration": "For decades, newspapers spun wild yarns of lights in the winter sky and ghostly abductions. Author Frank Edwards cemented the myth in his 1959 paranormal bestseller.",
        "visual_query": "vintage book pages",
        "visual_fallback": "old papers"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Yet Mountie records from the era reveal zero official reports of a missing village. Was the whole thing just an urban legend born from a lonely trapper's campfire story?",
        "visual_query": "royal canadian mounted police archive",
        "visual_fallback": "old documents"
      }
    ],
